<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Osaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1614">1614</a>–<a href="/years/1615">1615</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> was a series of campaigns in 1614–1615 in which the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>, held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> family. The castle fell and the Toyotomi line was destroyed. This removed the last rival source of legitimacy and completed Tokugawa control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka.jpg" alt="Siege of Osaka" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Utagawa Yoshitora · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osakarakuzyou_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> died in 1598, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> came to be governed by the Council of Five Elders, among whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> held the most authority. After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600, Ieyasu essentially took control of Japan and abolished the council. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> was established in 1603 with its capital at Edo. Hideyoshi's son Toyotomi Hideyori and his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono</a> were allowed to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>, Hideyoshi's residence. Ieyasu wanted a stable regime under his own clan, and the Toyotomi clan, led by Hideyori under the influence of Yodo-dono, was the only remaining obstacle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In November 1614 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> led 164,000 men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, and the siege began on 19 November. The Sanada-maru, an earthwork barbican defended by Sanada Yukimura with 7,000 men, repelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s armies repeatedly. Ieyasu then turned to artillery and tunnelling miners, and from 8 January 1615 he had the castle bombarded. The stone bases of Japanese castles withstood the guns of the time, but fire aimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono</a>'s quarters killed two of her maids and terrified her.</p><p>Peace talks followed, and on 21 January Ieyasu issued a document, sealed with blood from his finger, promising that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> would not be found guilty and that Hideyori's income would stay the same. The outer moat was filled in.</p><p>By April 1615 news came that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> were again recruiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> and trying to stop the filling of the moat, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> ordered Hideyori to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. Fighting resumed in the summer campaign, with battles at Kashii on 26 May and Dōmyōji on 2 June. The decisive Battle of Tennōji came with Tokugawa forces of around 155,000 led by the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a>. Mistakes on both sides almost changed the result, but Sanada Yukimura was killed by Nishio Munetsugu, which destroyed the Toyotomi army's morale. Hideyori's own force sallied out too late and was driven back, and the castle was soon ablaze and under artillery fire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The siege ended the last major armed opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>. In July 1615 the era name was changed from Keichō to Genna, apparently to mark the end of civil war since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a>, and the end of the conflict is sometimes called the Genna Armistice. Furuta Oribe, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who had been in league with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> and plotted to set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> on fire, was ordered to commit seppuku on 11 June.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Osaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
